\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} We classify HS advantage evidence into three tiers: \emph{cross-validated}\footnote{In our usage, \emph{cross-validated} means the result survives nested 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test fold, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. \emph{Exploratory} ($\dagger$) results pass HB on the holdout split but lack nested CV validation due to sample constraints.} (nested CV pass; 7--8B models), \emph{significant} (HB pass without nested CV; QwQ-32B), and \emph{exploratory} ($^\dagger$, small sample or narrow coverage; R1-32B). Per-model details follow in \cref{sec:layer-results,sec:hs-text,sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained~\cref{app:ot7b_details}, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Classification requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings (\cref{tab:k_ablation}). Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the crossing-rate composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers across all four models: L0 for R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep; \cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}) and OT-7B, L2 (3\%) for QwQ-32B, and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. The three threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge to the same deep layer, L63 (98\%) for both 32B models, consistent with their shared structure, though not mathematically guaranteed. Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects layers that vary across data splits and models (7--98\% depth under 60/20/20; \cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}), itself demonstrating that layer selection is a consequential methodological choice. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{CR-comp.}$^a$ & \textbf{Thresh.}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(thresh.)} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec}$^c$ \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L0 (0\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +20.1 \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +21.9 \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & L2 (3\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.005$ & +19.2 \\
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +15.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,Crossing-rate composite (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate). $^b$\,Consensus of threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). $^c$\,HS$-$text precision difference (pp) at threshold-based layer (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Rightmost $p$: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at threshold-based layer (e.g., QwQ-32B CR-composite layer: $p{=}0.83$, non-significant). $\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Blue dashed: CR-composite layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); orange dash-dot: threshold-based layer (consensus of threshold-based metrics). For R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep), crossing rate drives CR-composite selection to L0 while threshold-based metrics retain L14. At 32B, CR-composite selections cluster near input layers while threshold-based metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at CR-composite layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. At threshold-based layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B, R1-8B; nested CV), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$, and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (residualized gap below MDE). The $\dagger$ marks sample-limited test sets ($n \leq 20$) where effect sizes are descriptive rather than confirmatory. Had we reported only CR-composite-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact. Random-label controls on QwQ-32B and R1-8B confirm that threshold-based deep-layer selection requires genuine safety labels; the geometric shallow-layer bias is Qwen-specific: retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B (\cref{app:random_cp}). AUROC, excluded from our primary metrics because it is threshold-free (\cref{sec:evaluation}), confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\Delta$AUROC +12.1 to +15.1pp; \cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose the threshold-dependent layer selection bias.\looseness=-1

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 3 & +17.5 & +20.1 & [+12.4, +28.3] & $-$7.9 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \checkmark \\[2pt]
QwQ$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

The HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\dagger$ (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\cref{app:split_half}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d$\rightarrow$PCA 384d) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB; \cref{app:e5_mistral}), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision); R1-8B shows adverse FPR direction (+1.2pp). This forms a monotonic encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\to$ E5-Mistral-4096d$\to$384d (2/4) $\to$ BGE-large-1024d (1/4), with the precision advantage narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--12pp. The HS advantage is thus a function of text encoder capacity (\cref{fig:forest_precision}); layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy,\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$; at $W{\geq}3$, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$). Adding a quadratic position term $(t/T)^2$ does not change significance conclusions for the 7--8B models. However, QwQ-32B's advantage is fully absorbed by the quadratic term (\cref{tab:position_quadratic}), suggesting its residualized advantage contains a non-linear position component. Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (24.3pp drop at $W{=}3$ vs.\ 16.5pp at $W{=}15$).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested 5-fold CV confirms the HS precision advantage for both 7--8B models (10/10 folds; \cref{app:nested_cv}), with shrinkage of 13--25\% relative to holdout estimates. OT-7B nested CV selects L21--27 vs.\ holdout L16 vs.\ CR-composite L0; the +21.9pp holdout estimate is an upper bound. R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage; at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality prevents significance. QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). False alarm rates remain 11--50\%, so current methods characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

\paragraph{Exploratory 32B analysis.} Both 32B results are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$ precludes nested CV). LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks but do not substitute for independent validation. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); training method is one plausible differentiator. Replication with $n{\geq}50$ traces is needed.\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence ($\dagger$\,sample-limited, $n \leq 20$; MDE at 80\% power: 8.6--9.5pp, \cref{app:power_analysis}; all conclusions exploratory). R1-32B provides limited early identification with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. HB: \checkmark{} = passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\ margin: HS BA minus position baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B suggests commitment 11--13 steps early (+14.2pp over position baseline) but covers only 25\% of traces ($W{=}1$); the precision effect (+15.4pp, CI width 13.9pp) falls below MDE at 80\% power. QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage but negative HS lead time; the precision effect (+19.2pp, CI width 24.2pp) exceeds MDE (8.6--9.5pp; \cref{app:power_analysis}) but the position baseline achieves higher BA (0.832 vs.\ 0.796), and the HS precision margin over position (+4.5pp, $p{=}0.133$; \cref{app:position_residual}) is not significant at $n{=}19$. The divergence suggests per-model validation is needed.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}).\looseness=-1

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_forest_precision.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of HS precision advantage over text probes. Blue: vs.\ MiniLM-384d; orange: vs.\ BGE-1024d. Solid markers: survives Holm-Bonferroni; hollow: does not. All 384d comparisons are significant (strong to qualified evidence); against BGE-1024d, only OT-7B (strong) survives correction.}
\label{fig:forest_precision}
\end{figure}

CP-aligned probe score trajectories (\cref{fig:trajectory} in Appendix) confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point, with OT-7B showing the clearest separation and R1-32B the weakest.\looseness=-1

\enlargethispage{10\baselineskip}
\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\cref{app:negative_details}). The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, which excludes change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods respectively.\looseness=-1

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}.

\textit{Attack characterization.} Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts}, with longer chains increasing circumvention probability~\cite{selfjailbreak}. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful,turpin2023language} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff,hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak,hu2026refusal_dynamics,ahrend2026safer_traces}.

\textit{Detection.} CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking but optimization pressure promotes obfuscation~\cite{baker2025monitoring}. CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile~\cite{korbak2025cot_monitorability}. GuardReasoner~\cite{xiang2025guardreasoner} trains a dedicated reasoning-based guard model. \citet{schulz2025roadmap} proposes a structured safety-case framework grounded in CoT monitoring, formalizing the deployment context our probes would serve.

\textit{Mitigation.} SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and Chain-of-Guardrails~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} proposes guardrail insertion during reasoning. None of these conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{azaria2023internal} demonstrate that internal states encode whether a model's output is truthful, motivating representation-based monitoring. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers and argue it originates in pre-training rather than alignment; our temporal probes target a distinct phenomenon (commitment transitions during reasoning) that is absent from pre-training data. Meanwhile, \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, \citet{turner2023activation} steer behavior via activation addition without optimization, and \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal category-specific refusal directions, \citet{templeton2024scaling} extract interpretable features at scale, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026}, HiddenDetect~\cite{jiang2025hiddendetect}, SafeInt~\cite{safeint2025}, and RCS~\cite{rcs2026} extend hidden-state monitoring to proactive detection, vision-language models, intervention, and contrastive scoring respectively. \citet{zhao2026safeprobing} probe safety awareness during decoding; \citet{chia2025probing_subspaces} extract latent subspaces separating safe from adversarial states. These methods assume external input manipulation and operate at the token level. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} confirm layer-wise safety signal; \citet{zhang2025reasoning_right} probe hidden states for self-verification beyond safety.
Concurrently, \citet{chan2025probing} demonstrate that linear probes on the \emph{final layer} (residual stream, PCA-50) predict alignment outcomes with strong F1, establishing separability of safe/unsafe reasoning. However, their single-layer, single-metric evaluation (logistic regression on the last layer, evaluated by F1 and PR-AUC alone) does not examine whether different layers yield different conclusions, the central question of our work. Our five-metric taxonomy and full-depth layer sweep reveal that metric choice alone can reverse the modality comparison (\cref{sec:layer-results}), a vulnerability that applies to any fixed-layer design including theirs. Their sentence-level temporal segmentation and our token-level windowing are complementary: they establish that early segments suffice for outcome prediction; we show that the \emph{reliability} of such predictions depends on both the layer and the metric used to select it. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Causal abstraction~\cite{geiger2023causal} formalizes when high-level causal models faithfully describe neural computation, grounding use of internal representations. Linear probing spans layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding}, sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing}, and surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}. Control tasks~\cite{hewitt2019control} distinguish structure from memorization (we adopt these; \cref{app:control}). Information-theoretic and causal formulations address probe complexity~\cite{voita2020information,pimentel2020information,ravfogel2020null,ravfogel2022rlace,belrose2023leace,elazar2021amnesic}.\footnote{The Tuned Lens~\cite{belrose2023tunedlens} offers an alternative layer-wise analysis but assumes next-token prediction, misaligned with binary CP classification.} \citet{marks2024geometry} find emergent linear structure in LLM truth representations, consistent with our safety commitment separability. That information distributes non-uniformly across layers is well established~\cite{tenney2019bert,jawahar2019bert}. Cross-layer prediction consistency has been exploited for efficiency: \citet{kaya2019shallow} identify ``overthinking'' via cross-layer disagreement, and \citet{zhou2020bert} formalize patience-based early exit. We repurpose this for safety probe layer selection (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}). Our contribution is orthogonal: the known layer-wise information gradient interacts with metric choice to produce systematic selection bias in safety probes. Most directly, \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations; our metric bias analysis provides a complementary mechanism: shallow-layer probes exploit compressed representations that inflate crossing rate, a geometric effect consistent with text-leakage at layers where representations have not yet diverged from surface features. \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} apply hidden-state geometry to step-level hallucination detection concurrently with our work. \citet{damirchi2026truth} study internal representation trajectories for layer-wise truth encoding. Concurrently, \citet{chrabaszcz2026monitoring} monitor probe trajectories across reasoning steps to characterize reasoning dynamics; our work additionally shows that metric-dependent layer selection can reverse probe conclusions, a confound absent from their analysis. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis, a practice we show can reverse conclusions (\cref{sec:layer-results}). \citet{meyoyan2026bertology} propose token- and layer-selective probes for efficient classification, complementing our finding that layer choice is not merely an efficiency concern but a validity one. \citet{pais2026linear_probe_scaling} show that multi-layer ensembling recovers performance where single-layer probes fail, a complementary solution to the sensitivity problem we diagnose.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Alternative Detection Approaches.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

